# =========================================================================
# AN ISA SOUND CARD IS INVISIBLE TO THE DRIVER VM. THIS IS A LIMITATION,
# RECORDED AS A TEST SO IT CANNOT BE REDISCOVERED THE EXPENSIVE WAY.
#
# The experiment this project exists for is "attach a sound card the kernel has
# never heard of, ask the model to write a driver, hear a sound". The FIRST card
# tried was QEMU's `-device adlib` — OPL2 FM synthesis behind two I/O ports, no
# DMA, no descriptors, the least machinery between a model and a noise. It was
# expected to be the easy one. It is instead the impossible one, and not for any
# reason to do with audio:
#
#   adlib is an ISA device. It has no PCI configuration space at all.
#
# drivers/pci/pci.c publishes a device node per PCI function, so an ISA device is
# published by nobody. tools/dvm_tools.c's resolve_target() then requires the
# name to be a REGISTERED node AND to parse as "pci<bb>:<dd>.<f>" before it will
# derive a sandbox from BARs. An ISA card fails both halves, so driver_targets
# cannot offer it and driver_run cannot be pointed at it. There is no primitive
# for "grant me ports 0x388-0x389": every port window in the VM's policy is
# derived from a BAR, and there is no BAR.
#
# A live model was asked to drive one. It did everything right — enumerated the
# whole bus, filtered by class 4, checked device_list, checked audio_status —
# found nothing, and correctly told the operator the machine had no sound card
# rather than inventing one. See vm/transcripts/adlib.log.
#
# WHAT THIS CASE ASSERTS, and why each line is here rather than a comment:
#   * with an OPL2 card attached, the PCI bus is UNCHANGED (6 functions, none
#     audio-class) — so "the model found nothing" was the truth, not a bug in
#     the enumeration;
#   * the device tree gains no audio row, so the device model does not know
#     about it either;
#   * the audio service still says it can play nothing, which is the honest
#     answer and the one the model was given.
#
# WHEN THIS CASE FAILS, THAT IS PROBABLY GOOD NEWS. If someone teaches the
# kernel to publish legacy/ISA devices as targetable nodes with an explicit port
# window, this case starts failing and should be REWRITTEN, not deleted: the new
# assertion is that adlib IS offered by driver_targets. The comment above is the
# specification for that work.
#
# Syntax is documented in boot.case.
# =========================================================================

name: audio-isa: an ISA sound card cannot be a driver target
description: -device adlib attaches an OPL2 card that has no PCI config space, so the kernel cannot offer it to the driver VM at all
timeout: 45
nic: user,model=e1000

# No build-cflags: this must be the kernel `make` produces.

# `-audiodev none` so no host audio device is needed and the case behaves the
# same on any machine. adlib is an ISA device, so it takes no PCI slot argument.
qemu-extra: -audiodev none,id=snd0 -device adlib,audiodev=snd0

ready: ^>
ready [offline]: \[net init failed

# ---- the PCI bus is exactly what it is with no card at all --------------
# Six functions: host bridge, ISA bridge, IDE, ACPI, VGA, e1000. Attaching the
# sound card added nothing, because it is not on this bus. The count is asserted
# as well as the absence, so this cannot pass by the scan having failed.
expect-re: ^pci: 00:00\.0  8086:1237  class 06:00$
expect-re: ^pci: 00:03\.0  8086:100e  class 02:00$
expect-re: ^pci: 6 device\(s\)$
check: pci_enumeration count=6

# No PCI function of any audio class exists. `class 04:` is what pci.c prints
# for base class 4 (multimedia), and it must not appear for any subclass — an
# OPL2 chip is audio hardware by any reasonable definition and is still absent
# from the only namespace the driver VM can reach.
forbid-re: ^pci: .* class 04:

# ---- so the honest answer is the one the model was given ----------------
# Before the device tree, because `expect` is ordered and the audio service
# reports during driver init, well ahead of the tree dump.
expect-re: ^audio: no output driver is registered - nothing can play a sound yet$
forbid-re: ^audio: sink

# ---- and the device model has no audio node either ----------------------
expect: --- device tree ---
check: device_tree count=13
forbid-re: ^\s*\[\s*\d+\] \S+\s+audio\s

# ---- nothing in the image went looking for it --------------------------
# No program ran, so no port was poked. If a future change lets the VM reach
# legacy ports, it must still not do so unasked at boot.
forbid: [dvm
forbid-re: ^ac97:
forbid-re: \bOPL\b
forbid-re: \badlib\b

# ---- and the machine is otherwise completely normal --------------------
expect-re: ^  tools  : \d+ registered
forbid-re: \bpanic\b
forbid: heap corruption
forbid: kfree: double free
forbid-re: ^\[fault
